  "body": "Part of #4023. The design record `docs/decisions/4024-path-kernel-design.md` (PR #4030, merged) has the side-branch plan.\n\n## The maintainer's words\n\nQuestion dialog, 2026-11-25, on \"a side-branch rule in `AGENTS.md` first, and protect `side/path-kernel`\": \"Yes, both\".\n\nDelivery of the path kernel (question dialog, 2026-11-25): a side branch and one switch. The old behaviour stays on `main` until the new kernel is proven. No early `P1` patches go into the old planners.\n\n## What to do\n\nAdd a short rule with a stable name (**Side branch**) to `AGENTS.md`, in the \"Pull Request Law\" or next to **Overlapping issues stack**. Take the content from the section \"Side-branch plan\" of the record. The rule says:\n\n- what a side branch is, and that only the maintainer permits one. `side/path-kernel` is the one that exists.\n- A sub-PR targets the side branch, has one issue, opens as a draft, gets the quick pass and the collaborator review as each other PR, and ends with `Refs #N` (a merge into a side branch does not close an issue).\n- `main` is merged into the side branch (never a rebase, never a force-push), after each related merge batch and at least each week. A conflict in the same logic stops and goes to the maintainer.\n- The gates: `post-merge.yml` covers `main` only. Each sub-PR runs the quick gate, the example catalog suites and the light lane. The side branch gets a local batch gate after each sync, inside the build limit.\n- The switch-over to `main` is one PR that the maintainer approves. No agent merges it without his word.\n- The branch is protected against a force-push and a deletion.\n- The lane and in-flight limits count a sub-PR as each other PR.\n\nUpdate the skills that the rule touches only where a step would be wrong without it (`merge-ready`, `work-issue`): one or two lines each.\n\n## Done means\n\nThe rule is on `main`. The PR body has `Process change: yes`. The PR does not change `.claude/PROCESS_VERSION`.\n",
